1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.603
In this lesson, you will learn about different environments for running code,

2
00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:06.773
local execution, containers, and sandboxes like micro VMs.

3
00:00:06.973 --> 00:00:08.675
And you will learn how to choose

4
00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:10.377
the right one for your use case.

5
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.412
Alright, let's go.

6
00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:15.215
Executing LLM generated code can be done in different environments.

7
00:00:15.915 --> 00:00:18.818
You can run the code locally or use Docker containers.

8
00:00:19.219 --> 00:00:21.621
Another option is to use a sandbox environment.

9
00:00:22.222 --> 00:00:24.057
You might ask, what are the

10
00:00:24.057 --> 00:00:26.192
criteria for choosing one approach over another?

11
00:00:26.359 --> 00:00:28.161
It comes down to a lot of

12
00:00:28.161 --> 00:00:30.163
factors you need to consider for your agents.

13
00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.866
One of the challenges is security. Why?

14
00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:35.168
Because if we just run LLM

15
00:00:35.168 --> 00:00:37.470
generated actions directly, what can go wrong?

16
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.473
An attacker might read sensitive data from the system

17
00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:42.842
or even inject malicious code.

18
00:00:43.343 --> 00:00:45.845
The LLM can accidentally expose your API keys

19
00:00:45.945 --> 00:00:48.248
or a malicious code could crash the entire app.

20
00:00:48.648 --> 00:00:52.952
And there are also all the scaling issues and countless other vulnerabilities.

21
00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:55.755
If you are using Claude Code or Cursor,

22
00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:59.459
you're familiar with the approach of running LLM-generated code locally.

23
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.462
When using coding assistants, you expect the agent to run code

24
00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:04.731
and edit files on your machine.

25
00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.300
But local execution has important limitations

26
00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:09.502
and it doesn't provide strong security.

27
00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:12.405
The problem is that there's not a full isolation.

28
00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:14.808
If the model generates unwanted code,

29
00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:18.111
it can then run and read or modify anything on your machine.

30
00:01:18.611 --> 00:01:20.880
And even beyond security, it has limits.

31
00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:23.283
Your laptop's resources run out quickly.

32
00:01:23.883 --> 00:01:26.786
Environments might get messy with conflicting packages,

33
00:01:26.786 --> 00:01:29.889
and you can't easily let other users use your agent at scale.

34
00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.492
Local execution can quickly run into limits

35
00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.494
if you want to build something in production.

36
00:01:34.794 --> 00:01:37.897
Lovable, Rork, or many other popular agent techs up today,

37
00:01:37.897 --> 00:01:40.900
from deep research to coding agents, all utilize sandboxed

38
00:01:40.967 --> 00:01:43.770
solutions to scale to thousands of users.

39
00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:47.674
One option for agentic environments is Docker or containers in general.

40
00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:50.477
Docker provides operating system level isolation

41
00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:53.680
by running code in separate processes using namespaces.

42
00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:57.083
That makes it more isolated than local code execution.

43
00:01:57.283 --> 00:02:00.553
However, all containers still share the same host kernel.

44
00:02:00.954 --> 00:02:03.756
This means if even one of them is compromised,

45
00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.558
there's a potential path for an

46
00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.660
attacker to escalate and reach the host.

47
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:11.464
This risk becomes more relevant when running multiple instances

48
00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:14.467
of untrusted code in multiple containers simultaneously.

49
00:02:14.467 --> 00:02:17.437
By default, container syscalls go straight to the Host Kernel,

50
00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:19.439
which means bigger attack surface.

51
00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:21.474
One option to make Docker safer

52
00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:23.543
is to therefore reduce the attack surface.

53
00:02:23.743 --> 00:02:25.712
For that, we can use for example gVisor,

54
00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.515
an open source user space kernel from Google.

55
00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:30.817
gVisor jails the container, meaning we

56
00:02:30.817 --> 00:02:33.219
add restrictions on what it can access.

57
00:02:33.419 --> 00:02:36.222
It implements much of the Linux syscall interface itself,

58
00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.224
practically intercepting syscalls.

59
00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:40.593
It is a middleman.

60
00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:43.496
The container talks to gVisor first, not directly to the Host Kernel.

61
00:02:43.796 --> 00:02:45.598
There are tradeoffs of gVisor.

62
00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:48.902
Some performance overhead, occasional compatibility gaps,

63
00:02:49.002 --> 00:02:51.204
and different debugging or tracing tools.

64
00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:54.007
And even gVisor is still not 100% safe.

65
00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.842
It can still be compromised,

66
00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:58.244
because gVisor still depends on the Host Kernel.

67
00:02:58.244 --> 00:03:00.847
Its complex implementation vulnerabilities

68
00:03:00.847 --> 00:03:02.949
in inner layers still can be exploited.

69
00:03:02.949 --> 00:03:05.218
So, we have a clear goal here.

70
00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:07.620
LLM generated code should never be able

71
00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:11.758
to compromise the host system. The good solution is sandboxes.

72
00:03:11.758 --> 00:03:15.762
Sandbox is a restricted isolated environment for running untrusted code.

73
00:03:15.762 --> 00:03:19.065
Sandboxes minimize any impact on the host machine.

74
00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:23.069
Compared to containers, sandboxes provide real process isolation,

75
00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:28.069
making them a common foundation for running untrusted or LLM-generated workloads safely.

76
00:03:28.374 --> 00:03:33.079
Implementations of sandboxes include OS level isolation, web assembly,

77
00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:38.079
or lightweight microVMs such as Firecracker, QEMU, or cloud hypervisor.

78
00:03:39.118 --> 00:03:42.622
One type of implementation of sandbox solution

79
00:03:42.622 --> 00:03:45.425
that is very suitable for coding agents are MicroVMs.

80
00:03:46.025 --> 00:03:48.928
These are kernel-based lightweight virtual machines

81
00:03:48.928 --> 00:03:51.331
stripped of unnecessary hardware emulation,

82
00:03:51.331 --> 00:03:53.900
designed to start quickly and run efficiently.

83
00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:57.170
Each micro VM runs its own guest kernel,

84
00:03:57.170 --> 00:03:58.771
which provides stronger isolation

85
00:03:58.771 --> 00:04:01.074
while maintaining near native performance.

86
00:04:01.474 --> 00:04:05.478
AWS Firecracker is a widely used example of micro VM implementation.

87
00:04:05.778 --> 00:04:09.048
It powers services like AWS Lambda or Fargate.

88
00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.751
So if micro VMs provide stronger isolation,

89
00:04:11.751 --> 00:04:13.753
why don't we just use them everywhere?

90
00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.055
The answer is that there are important trade-offs.

91
00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:19.325
First, MicroVMs are harder to set up.

92
00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:22.128
Unlike Docker, you can't just run a single command.

93
00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:25.531
They require more engineering knowledge and manual configuration.

94
00:04:25.732 --> 00:04:28.234
The community and ecosystem are also smaller.

95
00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.336
You don't get the same tooling support

96
00:04:30.436 --> 00:04:32.839
or a public image registry like Docker Hub.

97
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.941
Scalability is another challenge.

98
00:04:34.941 --> 00:04:37.043
You need to manage the infrastructure yourself,

99
00:04:37.043 --> 00:04:39.045
including updates, orchestration,

100
00:04:39.045 --> 00:04:42.248
and addressing security vulnerabilities in your stack.

101
00:04:42.448 --> 00:04:44.450
MicroVMs can be a great solution,

102
00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.453
but running them directly creates some operational overhead

103
00:04:47.453 --> 00:04:49.856
that makes adoption harder for most teams.

104
00:04:50.256 --> 00:04:53.259
So yes, there are benefits of sandbox as a technology.

105
00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.762
But with the emergence of AI agents,

106
00:04:55.762 --> 00:04:58.064
we can also use benefits of sandbox providers.

107
00:04:58.431 --> 00:05:00.733
Companies like E2B, Deno or Modal

108
00:05:00.733 --> 00:05:03.036
provide sandbox environments with benefits

109
00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.737
on top of the sandbox technology.

110
00:05:05.038 --> 00:05:07.240
Instead of setting up MicroVMs yourself

111
00:05:07.240 --> 00:05:09.542
or maintaining your own observability.

112
00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:13.379
infrastructure. Providers give you production-ready sandboxes

113
00:05:13.413 --> 00:05:15.415
built specifically for AI agents.

114
00:05:15.515 --> 00:05:19.419
They can offer things like pre-built sandbox templates for different use cases,

115
00:05:19.519 --> 00:05:22.388
long-running sessions, or secrets and API management.

116
00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:25.591
And when you need to serve thousands or even millions of users,

117
00:05:25.692 --> 00:05:28.795
sandbox providers can offer the scalability that you need.

118
00:05:28.928 --> 00:05:31.731
They already manage the underlying orchestration,

119
00:05:31.764 --> 00:05:34.734
so you can focus on the agents, not on the infrastructure.

120
00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:38.738
Using sandboxes to run your code will be as easy as

121
00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:41.741
creating a sandbox instance and passing your code to its run.

122
00:05:42.241 --> 00:05:43.943
In the next lesson with Francesco,

123
00:05:43.943 --> 00:05:45.945
you will use E2B sandboxes in action

124
00:05:45.945 --> 00:05:48.414
to run the code your agent generates.
